// Settlements: hamlets and villages grown on good ground (see crates/worldgen/src/settlements).
//
// Every settlement is rolled from deep lists, and every roll leans on the settlement's
// character, so the lists stay coherent rather than shuffled: a rich, orderly village on a
// fertile river plain builds big stone-footed houses round a paved square with lamps
// along straight lanes; a poor, old hamlet on a cold hill is a few crooked huts on
// wandering paths with woodpiles and moss in the walls.
//
// LEVERS. Each settlement draws five levers, each 0 ..= 1, partly from its site and partly
// from the seed (the code says how; the wiki's settlements page records it):
//   wealth    fertile soil, fresh water and flat ground make it richer
//   age       how long it has stood: moss and cracks, crooked lanes, old kinds of house
//   order     planned or grown: straight lanes and aligned houses towards 1
//   crowding  how close the houses stand and how small their yards are
//   green     gardens, hedges, flowers and trees left standing
// Any entry below may `lean` on them: a lean of +1 on wealth doubles its weight in the
// richest places and drops it to nothing in the poorest; -0.5 halves it where it is
// richest. Leans multiply, so entries can lean on several levers at once.
//
// grid: one site is considered per square of `spacing` blocks; `chance` of the squares
//   hold one (mostly hamlets: see sizes), placed at least `margin` blocks inside the
//   square so a settlement never reaches into the next.
// avoid: biome tags no settlement is built in (rivers, lakes and their banks, crags and
//   terrain shapes are always avoided too).
// sizes: hamlets, villages and towns: how many houses, lanes and how long, the square's radius,
//   and the places they always or sometimes have (Well, Trader, Inn, Smithy, Stall,
//   Shrine, Field, Pen, Orchard, Woodyard). `crop_level` is the highest Farming level
//   (crops.ron) of what its fields grow: a field is never a shortcut past the skill, and
//   no crop or fruit rarer than uncommon is ever grown. Fields are drawn growing, never ripe.
//   `walls`: the chance it is walled (`rules.walls`) and how that leans; `smaller`: the
//   size of fewer houses it is laid out as where its ground will not take it.
// layouts: how lanes are laid out (Spokes, Line, Green, Scatter, Fork, Crossroads, Ring,
//   Riverside, Terraces); `water: true` only where fresh water is near.
// houses: the kinds of house, by size, storeys and form (Box, Round, AFrame, Courtyard,
//   ShopFront). Working buildings name their `work` (for the people who will work
//   there) and what stands `inside` (Oven, Millstone, Vats, Racks, Stalls, Bins, Smoke,
//   Altar, Pews, Boat, Counter; a house with fittings has one storey, as stairs run
//   round the walls where fittings stand); a house may have extras it `always` has, its own
//   `roofs`, the `sizes` that build it, the styles it is `not_in`, the `most` one place
//   builds, `stilts`, and `water: true` to stand only beside fresh water, and how many
//   `people` live in it, fewest and most (fuller where it is crowded; working buildings
//   mostly none). A-frames stay out of turf villages, where their grass roofs to the
//   ground read as banks of earth.
// roofs: how every roof shape leans, multiplying the weights styles give them (Gable,
//   Hip, HalfHip, Saltbox, Gambrel, Conical, Flat, Shed). Round houses take cones,
//   courtyard houses hips, A-frames steep gables, whatever was rolled, unless flat.
// extras: what a house may have besides walls and a roof, each by its own chance.
// props: what stands in yards and along lanes: one the code draws by its name, or one of
//   your own as `blocks` from its spot (y 1 the block on the ground); `water: true` ones
//   stand only on the bank of fresh water.
// centrepieces: the one great thing a place is known by, by `centrepiece` chance in
//   `sizes`: one the code draws by its name, made of its `parts` (log, leaves, stone,
//   worn, water, cloth, light, bell; `{wood}` the place's wood; any left out from the style),
//   or one of your own as `blocks` from its middle. `spot` Square stands it at the heart
//   of the square, the well beside it; Edge out by the end of a lane.
// Names now come from the culture families (cultures.ron), which also lean the lists
//   above to what each people builds. Every `lean` may name a lever (wealth, age, order,
//   crowding, green) or a culture axis (piety, martial, trade, craft, festivity, outlook,
//   hierarchy, lore).
// kana: how Japanese names join where the English runs a consonant into a vowel: a first
//   part ending in `kana` (and its English in `after`, if given) takes `into`'s kana for
//   ア, イ, ウ, エ or オ opening the last part (Sab + ir is Sabir: サブ + イル, サビル).
// styles: what a settlement is built of, by the biome it stands in; the first whose
//   biome tags match wins (an empty list matches anywhere). `woods` fill `{wood}` (the
//   main wood) and `{trim}` (a second wood, sometimes the same) in block names; every
//   material list is weighed per settlement, which keeps a few of its options, and each
//   house picks among those. `chimney`: what a chimney is built of, one block of the list
//   all the way up (Review, 7 Oct: one stone per chimney, never a mix). `roof_pieces`:
//   the shaped pieces a steep gable over four walls may be laid in (a `slope` and its
//   `ridge`, each facing four ways, by weight), on `pieced` of such roofs; the rest stay
//   whole blocks. `jetty`: the beam the storeys over the ground of a house of two or more
//   storeys stand a block out over the street on, and the chance one is (times 0.5 plus
//   the place's crowding). `row`: homes built wall to wall along a straight street (a
//   terrace), alike but for what is inside: a number drawn from `houses`, fewer where the
//   street or the homes wanted run out. A home of four walls on the ground starts one on
//   `chance` (times the place's crowding); the row stands at least the `storeys` its
//   place's size names (1 where it names none: town terraces are of town houses).
// people: who lives there and how their days go (see crates/worldgen/src/settlements/
//   people.rs). `jobs`: each building whose work or kind is `at` takes `count` of a
//   species (by `chance`), living above the shop when it has room, else in the home
//   nearest. `guards` by size, taking their `routines` in turn. `folk` fill the rest of
//   every home by weight (`work` sends them to the nearest building of that work or kind,
//   and they are drawn only where there is one; `alone: false` never the first in a
//   home). `routines` cut the day into slots `until` a share of it (0 sunrise, 0.667
//   sunset, 1 the next sunrise), each drawing one place a day among those the settlement
//   has (Sleep, Home, Yard, Work, Square, Well, Inn, Shrine, Neighbour, Gate, Patrol,
//   Wander), leaning on the levers. Each person's day runs early or late by up to
//   `jitter`; they move to another spot of a place after `linger` seconds; a newcomer
//   takes the place of someone who died after `newcomer` days (at least a quarter; the
//   world's save keeps who died), and a settlement takes `newcomers_per_day` at most.
//   `shrine`: the work or kind of building Shrine sends people to. `live`: how the server
//   keeps them (blocks and seconds): alive within `active` of a player, gone past
//   `release`, let go past `forget`; ways searched `margin` round the buildings, at most
//   `search` cells a way and `search_per_tick` a tick, a way not found from one cell to
//   another not searched again for `failed_for`; `stuck` without getting nearer, the way is found again, and with none,
//   looked for again after `no_way_retry`; patrols stop `patrol_pause` at each stop.
//   Guards within `help` of a villager struck come (looked for every `call_every`), find
//   their way to the striker every `repath` and go straight at them within `handoff`; one
//   that comes no nearer for `give_up` breaks off, heals and goes home for `calm`. `scale`:
//   each person's size by their genes', from the first to the second; `spots`: how far from
//   a place's middle they stand there. `react`: a player within `greet` is greeted (they
//   stop, turn and hum for `greet_for`, once in `greet_every`); a monster or a hunter
//   within `fear` sends them home crying out for `afraid` (guards stay); rain of at least
//   `rain` sends them home from the `shelter` places and from work at the `outdoor` works;
//   two neighbours (no guards) within `chat` who see each other stop and talk for
//   `chat_for`, each again no sooner than `chat_every` (`chat` no farther than `greet`);
//   a player one of them sees in their home at night is told to leave with a cry, each
//   again no sooner than `intruder_every`. One who likes a food (their kind's
//   `diet.favourite`: children) and sees a player within `treat` holding it comes to stand
//   by them while it is held; given one, they want another no sooner than `treat_every`.
(
    grid: (spacing: 1024, chance: 0.45, margin: 160),
    // site: where a settlement stands (design wiki, settlements, draft 60).
    //   temperature: none outside it (°C); mild: as likely as anywhere inside it, the chance
    //     falling towards the edges of `temperature` to `taper` of that.
    //   water: fresh water (a river or lake, not the sea) within this many blocks of its
    //     middle, or no settlement; bank: blocks every building keeps from water.
    //   fertility: the least average fertility of the ground under its buildings; `poor`
    //     lowers it in biomes with a tag.
    //   height: over sea level; relief: the most its ground rises and falls within `reach`
    //     of its middle; firm: blocks round its middle that must be dry land; caves: how far
    //     it keeps from sinkholes and ravines.
    //   step: blocks between the spots weighed in its square; tries: how many of the best
    //     are tried; others: how many of the next best are laid out in turn where the best
    //     will not lay out (its climate rolled once, on the best); score: what a spot scores (luck, less its spread and relief, more for
    //     water, less per block over sea level); where water is needed, a closer look for
    //     creeks goes through `look` times `tries` of the best, sampling every `creek` blocks.
    //   glade: no trees within glade.0 blocks of a house, fewer out to glade.1, glade_trees.0
    //     of them left past the treeless ring and glade_trees.1 more where it is green.
    //   eaves: crowns reaching past the ring are cut back over each building and this many
    //     blocks round it (0 to 8).
    //   levels: gentler land first (biome levels, P15; design draft 95): up to biome level
    //     levels.0 as likely as anywhere, levels.1 of that in harsher biomes. Above level 5,
    //     not 3: the desert, jungle and mangrove villages (levels 4 and 5) carry chickpea,
    //     rice and taro, and at 3 the nearest taro field moved from 7 to 25 squares out on
    //     seed 42 (regional_crop_reach).
    site: (
        temperature: (-10.0, 36.0), mild: (0.0, 26.0), taper: 0.25,
        water: 48, bank: 3,
        fertility: 0.3, poor: {"cold": 0.15, "desert": 0.15},
        height: (3, 90), relief: 14, reach: 48, firm: 24, caves: 150,
        step: 16, tries: 6, others: 2, look: 6, creek: 4, score: (luck: 3.0, spread: 1.2, relief: 0.25, water: 3.0, height: 0.02),
        glade: (5, 16), glade_trees: (0.08, 0.1), eaves: 1,
        levels: (5, 0.7),
    ),
    // rules: the rest of the numbers planning reads.
    //   spawn_settlement_within: a new player finds a settlement within this many blocks of
    //     where they spawn: of the squares in reach, the nearest that can hold one always
    //     does, unless one already stands in reach (design, 7 Oct: P21's early quests send
    //     them there). 0 promises none.
    //   spawn_tries: that square looks at this many times `site.tries` spots, and keeps
    //     this many at most to lay out, best first (1 to 32). spawn_salts: it lays each out
    //     with this many draws (other lanes, the well elsewhere), then as many again as the
    //     smallest place, before it is left empty (1 to 32). Each draw levels lots and
    //     looks for water round their banks, so these bound the spawn's planning.
    //   names_apart: no two settlements within this many blocks of each other share a name
    //     (design, draft 81).
    //   levers: how each lever is drawn: `luck` times the mean of `rolls` rolls, plus shares
    //     of fertility, fresh water near, flat ground (flat below `steep`), the wealth drawn
    //     first, and forest (undergrowth counting `undergrowth` of it); clamped to 0 ..= 1.
    //   lanes: half the width of side lanes; how much lanes bend (orderly, and more where
    //     not); blocks per step walked, the most a step rises, how far lanes keep apart; a
    //     green's radius (square times green.0, plus green.1); a scattered place's square
    //     and how often its paths branch; a ring lane's distance past the square (and more
    //     where not crowded), its wobble and the most it rises; a terrace's step up; how far
    //     a riverside street looks for its water; `leave`: blocks a lane walks before it
    //     keeps `apart` from others; `growth`: side lanes grown when homes are still wanted
    //     after every pass, and their share of a lane's length; `centre`: the square grows
    //     to half a centrepiece plus centre.0, at most centre.1; `well`: blocks it stands past
    //     a centrepiece, and the most its ground rises, falls or stands off the square's;
    //     `rim`: a green's houses stand rim.0 past its square (and the gap), at least rim.1
    //     from its middle, in up to rim.2 tries.
    //   lots: blocks between buildings (and up to more where not crowded); spacing along a
    //     lane, tightened by `closer` each of `passes` passes; setback from the lane; yard
    //     kept clear; the most a floor stands off its lane (`road`); tries for each outlying
    //     building; the most the ground under a house rises and falls (more on stilts);
    //     `field`: where a place rolled neither field nor pen, the chance of a field (and more
    //     where green), else a pen; `outskirts`: outlying buildings stand past that share of a
    //     lane, up to that many blocks back; `farm`: the field or pen a place must have is
    //     looked for farm.0 times harder, past farm.1 of a lane, up to farm.2 back.
    //   specials: each special building's size, across and deep (every `step`), and the
    //     most its ground may rise and fall.
    //   houses: the inn's least storeys, width and depth, from houses `grand` wide; the
    //     trader's width and depth; houses `pitch.0` wide may have a low roof by pitch.1;
    //     order past `regular` lines houses up, past `middle_door` centres their doors;
    //     windows: the style's chance times windows.0 plus windows.1 times wealth, at most
    //     windows.2; `plinth`: the chance of a stone lowest course; `moss`: old walls'
    //     share of weathered blocks.
    //   dressing: blocks between things along lanes (and more where poorer, times the
    //     size's `lamps`), the chance each is a lamp (and more where richer), props in
    //     yards per building, the most per lane, boats and nets on the bank, and how far
    //     props keep from lanes (by a lane, in a yard).
    //   walls: for walled places (a size's `walls`): how far out from the buildings the wall
    //     runs (a ring round them all but fields, pens, orchards, woodyards and shrines),
    //     how high a palisade of logs and a stone wall stand, the wealth past which it is
    //     stone, and towers at the corners and every `towers.0` blocks, `towers.1` higher.
    //     A gate stands where each lane passes, with a lintel where the wall is over three;
    //     through a stone wall, `arches` arch it (the first whose `walls` holds the wall's
    //     stone, or that names none): an arch between the posts of a gate a block wide, a
    //     wide arch's halves springing from the posts of a wider one.
    //   fire: no flame of a settlement's own (`flames`, by tag) reaches what catches
    //     (`catch`): out a face at a time through open cells, `reach` steps, as fire
    //     catches in a home (fire.ron's reach is 1 to 4; this keeps the most). A roof of
    //     pieces that catch which a flame reaches is laid in `instead`; a flame that still
    //     reaches something is put out.
    rules: (
        spawn_settlement_within: 1024,
        spawn_tries: 8,
        spawn_salts: 8,
        names_apart: 4096,
        levers: (
            wealth: (luck: 0.45, fertility: 0.25, water: 0.2, flat: 0.1),
            age: (luck: 1.0, rolls: 2),
            order: (luck: 0.65, wealth: 0.35),
            crowding: (luck: 0.75, wealth: 0.25),
            green: (luck: 0.6, forest: 0.4),
            steep: 0.6, undergrowth: 0.6,
        ),
        lanes: (
            side: 0.8, wiggle: (0.06, 0.32), step: 3.0, rise: 2, apart: 3.5,
            green: (1.5, 2), scatter: 2, branch: 0.6,
            ring: (10.0, 4.0), wobble: 1.2, ring_rise: 6, terrace: (11.0, 4.0), riverside: 48,
            leave: 6.0, growth: (4, 0.6), centre: (5, 16), well: (3, 1), rim: (4.0, 8.0, 24),
        ),
        lots: (
            gap: (1, 3.0), spacing: (7.0, 6.0), closer: 0.7, passes: 3, setback: (1.5, 3.0), yard: (1, 2.0), road: 3, tries: 10, slope: 4,
            field: (0.25, 0.5), outskirts: (0.55, 4.0), farm: (5, 0.2, 8.0),
        ),
        specials: {
            "smithy": (width: (6, 8), depth: (5, 7), slope: 4),
            "field": (width: (7, 11), depth: (7, 13), step: 2, slope: 3),
            "pen": (width: (6, 10), depth: (6, 9), slope: 5),
            "orchard": (width: (9, 13), depth: (9, 13), slope: 3),
            "woodyard": (width: (4, 6), depth: (3, 5), slope: 5),
            "shrine": (width: (3, 3), depth: (3, 3), slope: 5),
            "stall": (width: (3, 3), depth: (2, 3), slope: 2),
            "centrepiece": (width: (3, 3), depth: (3, 3), slope: 3),
        },
        houses: (inn: (2, 8, 9), grand: 8, trader: ((5, 7), (5, 8)), pitch: (8, 0.45), regular: 0.45, middle_door: 0.6, windows: (0.6, 0.8, 0.95), plinth: 0.35, moss: 0.22),
        dressing: (every: (9.0, 14.0), lamp: (0.5, 0.4), yards: (0.5, 1.0, 2), per_lane: 6, banks: (1, 3), clearance: (0.4, 0.6)),
        walls: (margin: 4, height: (3, 4), stone: 0.5, towers: (24, 2), arches: [
            (walls: ["stone_bricks", "polished_andesite"], arch: "stone_brick_arch", wide: "stone_brick_wide_arch", wide_half: "stone_brick_wide_arch_half"),
            (arch: "cobblestone_arch", wide: "cobblestone_wide_arch", wide_half: "cobblestone_wide_arch_half"),
        ]),
        fire: (flames: ["fire"], catch: ["thatch", "wattle"], reach: 4, instead: Some((slope: "dark_oak_shingles", ridge: "dark_oak_shingle_ridge"))),
    ),
    // furnishings: blocks every settlement uses whatever its style. A package may name
    //   others; content without one leaves it out (no water or dirt: no settlements).
    furnishings: (
        torch: "torch", campfire: "campfire", table: "crafting_table", farmland: "farmland",
        water: "water", dirt: "dirt", planter: "herb_planter", anvil: "polished_basalt",
        // altar: chapels' and shrines' altars, which restore prayer (blocks tagged `altar`).
        barrel: "barrel", bell: "bell", altar: "stone_altar",
        flowers: [Tag("flower", 1)],
        bushes: [Tag("bush", 1)],
        canopies: [Name("red_terracotta", 1), Name("orange_terracotta", 1), Name("yellow_terracotta", 1), Name("white_terracotta", 1), Name("light_blue_terracotta", 1), Name("cyan_terracotta", 1), Name("lime_terracotta", 1), Name("magenta_terracotta", 1), Name("pink_terracotta", 1), Name("green_terracotta", 1), Name("blue_terracotta", 1)],
        compost: [Name("coarse_dirt", 1), Name("rooted_dirt", 1), Name("podzol", 1)],
        boulders: [Name("cobblestone", 1), Name("mossy_cobblestone", 1), Name("cobbled_andesite", 1)],
        // Fruit trees in blossom or unripe (a growing stage, never the ripe leaves), so an orchard
        // gives nothing until it grows: what it bears is its grower's.
        fruit: [Name("apple_leaves_1", 1), Name("apple_leaves_2", 2), Name("pear_leaves_1", 1), Name("pear_leaves_2", 1), Name("plum_leaves_2", 1), Name("hazelnut_leaves_2", 1)],
        hay: [Name("hay_bale", 1)],
        hanging: [Name("hanging_herbs", 1)],
        nets: [Name("fishing_net", 1)],
        sacks: [Name("sack", 1)],
        crates: [Name("crate", 1)],
        cloth: [Name("white_terracotta", 4), Name("light_blue_terracotta", 1), Name("red_terracotta", 1), Name("yellow_terracotta", 1), Name("pink_terracotta", 1), Name("light_gray_terracotta", 1)],
    ),
    // interiors: what stands in homes, by who lives there (crates/worldgen/src/settlements/
    //   interior.rs; P58b). A home is furnished once its people are known: a bed for each
    //   (upstairs first, head to a wall, back from the door; `counts.beds` at most), a table
    //   with a seat facing it for each and `guests` more (`seats` at most), `stores` against
    //   the walls, a corner of their `trade`, `lights`, cloth on the `walls` at head height,
    //   a `rug` by chance, a `cook`ing place where there is no hearth, and `tops` set on the
    //   table and each cupboard by chance. Counts and chances lean like the lists; nothing
    //   ever stands in front of the door or cuts a floor off from the door and the stairs.
    //   A roomier home holds more: past the first `roomy` columns of floor, one store more
    //   for every two `roomy` columns, and a light and a wall cloth for every three.
    //   pieces: each a `role` (Bed, Table, Seat, Store, Cook, Rug, Light, Wall, Top, Trade),
    //     its `block` (`{wood}` the furniture wood, `{dye}` the home's dye, `{glaze}` its
    //     glaze), weight and lean; a Trade piece names `who` it is for (a species, or the
    //     work where someone works). Seats, rugs, wall cloth and tops are never solid, beds
    //     are tagged `bed`, and every piece drops nothing or is a `furnishing`, so a village
    //     gives none away (loading checks each in every wood, dye and glaze).
    //   wood: the furniture wood where the place's wood is not in `woods`.
    //   dyes, glazes: one of each is drawn per home, leaning like the lists.
    interiors: (
        wood: "oak",
        woods: {
            "oak": "oak", "dark_oak": "dark_oak", "spruce": "dark_oak", "birch": "birch", "palm": "birch", "acacia": "oak",
            "baobab": "oak", "cherry": "birch", "cypress": "dark_oak", "jungle": "dark_oak", "mangrove": "dark_oak",
            "maple": "oak", "pine": "oak", "redwood": "dark_oak", "willow": "birch",
        },
        dyes: [
            (name: "white", weight: 3.0, lean: {"order": 0.3}),
            (name: "cream", weight: 3.0),
            (name: "ochre", weight: 1.5, lean: {"age": 0.3}),
            (name: "amber", weight: 1.0, lean: {"festivity": 0.5}),
            (name: "red", weight: 1.5, lean: {"festivity": 0.5, "martial": 0.3}),
            (name: "rust", weight: 1.5, lean: {"wealth": -0.3}),
            (name: "rose", weight: 1.0, lean: {"festivity": 0.5}),
            (name: "mulberry", weight: 0.6, lean: {"wealth": 0.6, "hierarchy": 0.5}),
            (name: "indigo", weight: 0.8, lean: {"wealth": 0.5, "lore": 0.3}),
            (name: "sky", weight: 1.0, lean: {"outlook": 0.4}),
            (name: "teal", weight: 0.8, lean: {"trade": 0.3}),
            (name: "green", weight: 1.5, lean: {"green": 0.5}),
        ],
        glazes: [
            (name: "earthen", weight: 4.0, lean: {"wealth": -0.3}),
            (name: "ivory", weight: 1.5, lean: {"wealth": 0.6}),
            (name: "cobalt", weight: 0.5, lean: {"wealth": 0.8, "trade": 0.5}),
        ],
        counts: (
            beds: 4, guests: 1, seats: 4,
            stores: (range: (1, 3), lean: {"wealth": 0.6}),
            trade: (range: (1, 2), lean: {"craft": 0.4}),
            walls: (range: (0, 2), lean: {"wealth": 0.4, "festivity": 0.4}),
            lights: (range: (0, 2), lean: {"wealth": 0.3, "festivity": 0.5}),
            rug: (chance: 0.45, lean: {"wealth": 0.8}),
            cook: (chance: 0.5, lean: {"wealth": 0.3}),
            tops: (chance: 0.55, lean: {"wealth": 0.4}),
            roomy: 12,
        ),
        pieces: [
            (role: Bed, block: "{wood}_bed"),
            (role: Table, block: "{wood}_table", weight: 4.0),
            (role: Table, block: "{wood}_desk", weight: 0.6, lean: {"lore": 0.6, "wealth": 0.3}),
            (role: Seat, block: "{wood}_chair", weight: 2.0, lean: {"wealth": 0.5, "order": 0.3}),
            (role: Seat, block: "{wood}_stool", weight: 2.0, lean: {"wealth": -0.5}),
            (role: Seat, block: "{wood}_bench", weight: 1.0, lean: {"crowding": 0.3, "hierarchy": -0.3}),
            (role: Seat, block: "{dye}_cushion", weight: 0.6, lean: {"festivity": 0.4, "lore": 0.3}),
            (role: Store, block: "{wood}_cupboard", weight: 2.0, lean: {"wealth": 0.4}),
